These are a short set of opinions on the books (or audiobooks) I have read over August 2025!

### ***Algorithms to Live by: The Computer Science of Human Decisions* by Brian Christian and Tom Griffiths**

This book explores how many of the common algorithms used in computer science actually have a relevance to real-world problem. These includes things like scheduling, sorting, knowing how much to do etc. It was insightful to see the crosses between these computational problems and how they appear in our daily lives more commonly than not.

Personally, as a computer scientist, I loved this book, needless to say. I think the authors both introduced, explained and conceptualised many computer science algorithms and ideas in an understandable way. As a student I remember the toils of going through algorithm and data structures classes, but in contrast this book had little of the verbosity and was quite a breeze to get through.

What I enjoyed the most is how the author drew links from our everyday problems to these algorithms used in computer science to point us that actually these computational problems appear more often that not. Simple things like creating a schedule for the week, or finding the optimal parking lot, or finding hobbies to do can all be framed as computational problems. The book goes through countless more examples and applications, and it is a great book for people to start viewing the world through the lenses of computational thinking.

### *The Beauty of Intolerance* by Josh McDowell and Sean McDowell

This book talks about the shifting of the idea of cultural tolerance within our society today. The dominant narrative now is that all lifestyles are equally valid. To be culturally tolerant is to approve each individual’s lifestyle choice. Addressing this, the book presents the paradoxical case that Christians should be intolerant of certain lifestyles, but yet should continue loving every individual regardless of their lifestyle.

The book presents this point by providing a few examples of difficult interactions with this undergirding narrative. It shows the difficulty in navigating interactions where there is the clash between the conservative and the modern. This is resolved by presenting Jesus as the model of how truth can be spoken in love, and beckons us to follow his example. It closes with real-world examples of how the cultural tolerance is so pervasive within our society today, from education, to government, to families and even the church.

This book has given me insight on the complexity of this issue of cultural tolerance. In particular, it has shown me the nuances of different viewpoints, and how seemingly small things actually highlight much deeper divergences. An example of this is how claims about science is taught to us to be “harder truths” than claims about morality. But most importantly, this book has inspired to learn to love other despite their worldviews, to be courageous to speak truth in love, and to grow in humility.

### *Supercommunicators* by Charles Duhigg

This book is about developing supercommunicator skills to improve communication and connection with others. The two key ideas to communicating better are, to identify the type of conversation taking place, and to leverage the power of questions. The three types of conversations are analytical, emotional, and identity-based, and recognising and matching the conversation is key to connection.

The author uses questions as the main tool for communication, as they are good for showing interest and building connection. To better this, one can prove to the other person that they are listening by reflecting and paraphrasing what the other person said back to them. After listening, sharing back vulnerability creates emotional contagion which leads to deepening connection. Finally, learning to ask deep questions can invite the other person to share their values and beliefs.

This book has brought some new perspective to me, as a person looking to improve how I interact with people. A skill which I will be practicing is learning to ask deep questions. I think this is important as deep questions lead to a deeper understanding of deeper things like values, even if the question does not appear deep at first. To do this I will be trying to reframe the way I ask questions. This book has taught me that being socially connected matters, as the biggest sign of success in life is the quality of relationships.

Mindset shifts make all the difference. A study — I believe — has shown, that seeing your weekend as a vacation, helps you to treat it like one. You end up enjoying it more, and become more mindful and present of it. Kind of like how you would have joy and anticipation when going on a fun road trip somewhere. Enjoy your weekend to the maximum!

